Brainier is the superlative degree of the English adjective brainy, which inherits the semantic core of brain (the organ of the head used metaphorically for intellect). The etymological path follows a typical Germanic development: a Proto-Germanic root for brain gives rise to Old English forms referring to the brain, then to Middle English and Early Modern English uses of brain as the base noun, then adjectives such as brainy appear with the suffixed -y, and the modern comparative brainier emerges from brainy by the regular comparative -er. The word family then yields cognates across West Germanic languages, illustrated by Dutch brein, German Gehirn, Danish hjerne, Norwegian hjerne, and Swedish hjärna, among others. The mainstream, citable etymologies anchor the entry in standard reference works and dictionaries.

